          ":question/worked-solution": "Compute each partial derivative while holding the other variables constant. In the $z$ derivative, both the coordinate prefactor and the argument depend on $z$, so use both the product rule and the chain rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y,z)&=z\\cdot \\cos{\\left(2 x z + y \\right)}\\cdot\\left(2 z\\right) \\\\ &=2 z^{2} \\cos{\\left(2 x z + y \\right)} \\\\ f_{y}(x,y,z)&=z\\cdot \\cos{\\left(2 x z + y \\right)}\\cdot\\left(1\\right) \\\\ &=z \\cos{\\left(2 x z + y \\right)} \\\\ f_{z}(x,y,z)&=\\sin{\\left(2 x z + y \\right)}+z\\cdot \\cos{\\left(2 x z + y \\right)}\\cdot\\left(2 x\\right) \\\\ &=2 x z \\cos{\\left(2 x z + y \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(2 x z + y \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the components in coordinate order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y,z)&=\\left(2 z^{2} \\cos{\\left(2 x z + y \\right)}\\right)\\,i \\\\ &+\\left(z \\cos{\\left(2 x z + y \\right)}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &+\\left(2 x z \\cos{\\left(2 x z + y \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(2 x z + y \\right)}\\right)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
